\section{Overview}

IT automatically creates separate Canvas rooms for all the course instances.
However, we want to merge them into one, so that all students can communicate
with each other.

IT creates the course instances 270 days before the course start.
This gives us time to email them as soon as the new course rooms pop up in
Canvas, months before any students are in the course.

That is what this script does: whenever there is more than one matching
course room in Canvas, it sends an email to IT and asks them to merge the
rooms.
<<[[merge-rooms.sh]]>>=
#!/bin/bash

<<environment>>

<<find the course rooms in [[course_rooms]]>>
if <<there is more than one course room>>
then
  <<send email to IT to merge Canvas rooms in [[course_rooms]]>>
fi
@

This is a script of its own, and not a part of [[datintro.sh]] (see
[[datintro.nw]]), because the two must run on different schedules.
The grading must run often, at times twice an hour.
This email must be sent seldom, so that we don't send another one before IT
has acted on the first.
When the two shared a script, the email part had to guard itself with a test
on the date, and that guard only worked as long as the script ran at most
once a day.
So every change of the grading schedule had to consider the emails too.

Now Cron decides when each of them runs (see Section~\ref{sec:scheduling})
and this script has no notion of time: every run checks the rooms and, if
there is more than one, sends the email.
That also lets us run it by hand to send the email right away.


\section{Environment}

Cron runs the script in a minimal environment, so we set up the same two
things as [[datintro.sh]] does: the credentials in [[~/.credentials]], and a
[[PATH]] that includes the locally installed Python commands, like
[[canvaslms]].
<<environment>>=
source ~/.credentials
PATH=${PATH}:~/.local/bin
export PATH
@


\section{Finding the course rooms}

The rooms to merge are those of the course round that starts this autumn and
the one that starts next spring.

We must ensure that we don't see old data from the [[canvaslms]] cache.
We use the [[--no-cache]] option to check the available course rooms.
Without it, it has happened that IT fixed it but we didn't see it due to the
cache.
<<find the course rooms in [[course_rooms]]>>=
YEAR=$(date +%y)
course_rooms=$(canvaslms --no-cache \
                  courses "DD1301.(HT${YEAR}|VT$(expr $YEAR + 1))" \
               | sed 's/\r$//')
@

We get one line per course room, so there is something to merge when there
is more than one line.
The [[$?]] must come right after the assignment above; it is the exit status
of that pipeline, which means that of [[sed]].
A failed lookup is caught by the line count instead: it yields no course
rooms, and the empty string is still only one line when we [[echo]] it.
<<there is more than one course room>>=
test "$?" = "0" -a "$(echo "${course_rooms}" | wc -l)" -gt 1
@


\section{The email}

We simply generate a message that we send to IT on the usual email address.
Each line of [[course_rooms]] is one room, with tab-separated columns, so we
split on newlines only when we loop over them.
<<send email to IT to merge Canvas rooms in [[course_rooms]]>>=
<<adjust IFS for the loop>>
echo "
Hej!

Kan ni slå ihop följande canvasrum till DD1301 HT${YEAR} (datintro${YEAR})?

$(for course in ${course_rooms}; do
    echo -n " - "
    echo "${course}" | cut -f 1,3- | sed "s/\t/ /g"
  done)

    Tack så mycket,

        Daniel
" | <<fix indents>> | mutt -s "Slå ihop canvasrum" it-support@kth.se
<<restore IFS>>
<<adjust IFS for the loop>>=
oldIFS="$IFS"
IFS=$'\n'
<<restore IFS>>=
IFS="$oldIFS"
@

Now, Noweb will indent the message, since the chunk is used inside the
[[if]] statement, two spaces in.
But we don't want an indented message.
So we remove exactly that indentation; if the chunk ever moves to another
depth, this must follow.
<<fix indents>>=
sed "s/^  //"
@


\section{Scheduling}
\label{sec:scheduling}

We run the script from Cron, at eight in the morning on days 1 and 15 of
the month:
\begin{verbatim}
00 08 1,15 1-6,8-12 * /bin/bash ${HOME}/merge-rooms.sh
\end{verbatim}

Twice a month means that IT should have had the time to merge the rooms
before we ask them about the same thing again.
We don't send during the vacation period, usually July, as then it might
pass more than two weeks before they can process the previous email.

We used to skip August and September too.
That was only because the grading runs twice an hour then, and this was a
part of the grading script.
